---
/**
 * Privacy: this website, Vekrun Personal, Live Share (the one feature that
 * goes through a Vekrun-operated gateway), Vekrun Cloud and Enterprise, and model providers.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro';
---
<Base title="Privacy" fullTitle="Privacy · Vekrun" description="This website uses no cookies, analytics or trackers. In Vekrun, your work stays on machines you control. You decide what reaches a model provider, and what Live Share relays when you turn it on.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Privacy" title="Your work stays on your machines." lede="No cookies or trackers on this site. In the product, your work stays local unless you send it somewhere: to a model provider you choose, or through Live Share when you turn it on." />
  <section class="rule pb-24 pt-14">
    <div class="container-site prose-site max-w-3xl text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="!mt-0">This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies, analytics or advertising scripts. Your browser stores only your theme choice, if you change it.</li>
        <li>Fonts load from this site. Nothing comes from third parties.</li>
        <li>Early access requests and the Cloud wishlist are emails you send to support@vekrunapp.com from your own email app. We keep the address you write from and what you choose to tell us, use them only to answer you and to send Vekrun Cloud updates if you asked for them, and never sell or share them. Reply “unsubscribe” at any time and we’ll remove you.</li>
        <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages, so GitHub may log requests under its <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">privacy statement</a>. Vekrun doesn’t see those logs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Vekrun Free</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No Vekrun account. Your code, projects and work don’t go through a Vekrun server unless you turn on Live Share.</li>
        <li>Projects, Work Items, Engineering Memory, logs and audit history stay on your machine. You can delete them at any time.</li>
        <li>Imported notes, such as an Obsidian vault, are read on your machine and never changed. Notes marked private are skipped.</li>
        <li>Usage reporting is off unless you turn it on.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="live-share">Live Share</h2>
      <p>Live Share is optional and off until you turn it on. It gives the app you’re building a permanent HTTPS link, and that link goes through a public gateway Vekrun operates.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What stays local.</strong> Your repository, files, Engineering Memory, agents and their logins. The link shows only the app you choose to share, and only while it runs.</li>
        <li><strong>What the gateway relays.</strong> Visitors’ requests to your link and the shared app’s responses. With Review Mode on, it also relays reviewer feedback to your Review Inbox: the comment, the page and the part of it they pointed at.</li>
        <li><strong>What the gateway keeps.</strong> Your link’s address and who may open it. If you set a password, the gateway checks it and keeps only its hash.</li>
        <li><strong>Your controls.</strong> Make the link public or password-protected, sign everyone out, stop sharing an app or turn Live Share off at any time. A public link is exactly that, so treat its address as public.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Vekrun Cloud and Enterprise</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Both are planned and not available. Vekrun Cloud would host workspaces and Factory execution for you; Enterprise would run on infrastructure your organization controls. Each will have its own privacy terms before anyone can use it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Model providers and connected services</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>An external model provider receives the prompts and code a job sends it, under your account and its terms. Local models keep that on your hardware.</li>
        <li>Your policies decide which providers are allowed.</li>
        <li>Trackers, Git hosts and monitoring services receive what the actions you authorize need.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
